Integer division as Java has it, in the wasm-gc target.

TeaVM divided with div_s, rem_s, div_u and rem_u, which trap where Java does
not: Integer.MIN_VALUE / -1 (and Long's) is MIN_VALUE in Java, and a zero
divisor throws ArithmeticException.  The quotient by -1 is now a negation,
behind a test of the divisor where it is not a constant, and with --strict,
which makes a null dereference and an index out of bounds Java's exceptions
too, a zero divisor throws ArithmeticException("/ by zero"); without it a
zero divisor still traps.  rem_s gives MIN_VALUE % -1 as 0, as Java does.

TeaVM's dependency analysis is told a division can throw it -- as a cast can
throw ClassCastException -- and so are divideUnsigned and remainderUnsigned,
or a catch around one would not see the exception's class, and a call on what
it caught (getMessage) would find no method in the class's table.

test/teavm/fixes/division, division_nonzero and unsigned.

